Consider this C# code:

```csharp
int total = 0;
Action add = () => total += 5;
Func<int> read = () => total;

add();
add();
Console.WriteLine(read());
```

It prints `10`. The `read` delegate was created while `total` was `0`, and `read` never changes `total`. Explain why `read()` still returns `10`.

In this C# code, `Counter.Report()` prints the counter's name:

```csharp
var a = new Counter("A");
Action viaGroup = a.Report;            // method-group conversion
Action viaLambda = () => a.Report();   // lambda

a = new Counter("B");

viaGroup();
viaLambda();
```

What do the two calls print, and why?
